  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nDry weather is expected until the return of rain chances Friday\nmorning. A push of warm advection in southwest low-level flow\nmay slightly steepen low lapse rates into Friday morning. Most \nensembles have convection firing in the 03Z to 06Z window, but \nfavoring points southwest of the forecast area. Accompanying \nthis, east OH and north WV may be on the northern fringe of a \ndecaying MCV that may increase confidence in convection \nslightly. \n\nAround 75% to 90% of guidance has the QPF maxima south of this \nregions OH/WV counties, but there is some spatial uncertainty. \nIn the 10% to 25% chance this warm frontal rain sets up into our\narea, HREF/RRFS maintain the off chance of a patchy 2\" to 4\" \narea in storm training Friday morning. Given the high confidence\nin the actual MCV passing south, morning arrival time, and \nmoist adiabatic profiles, the severe risk is low to none. Most\nensembles have the conditional, low probability flood threat \nwindow from around 4am to noon and favoring north WV and\nsoutheast Ohio.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nGuidance suggests a bit of a break in weather threats as a\nsurface high pressure attempts to push south from Ontario, but\nthis break will be brief before more severe/flood threats \narrive Sunday as the high pushes east. Ensembles generally agree\nin an west-east sliding low. This area may see a slow moving \nwarm front ahead of the system and a slow dragging cold front \nbehind. \n\nThe flatter the flow, the less of a chance for ascent and \nminimization of any threats. But should the upper shortwave \ntrend more amplified, there may be more ascent near the surface \nlow. This would increase excessive rainfall threats along the \nrelatively slow passing fronts and raise convective threats in \nthe warm sector. The conditional flood risk favors the whole \narea with a reasonable worst case scenario (10% chance) of a \nlocalized amount somewhere of 3 to 5 inches in a 48hr period. \nThe conditional severe threat increases from north to south, as \ndoes the distance into the warm sector. The primary threat would\nappear to be damaging wind at this time.",
